browserless-basics.body:
Headless Chrome as a service. Authenticate ONLY with the token as the `?token=` query parameter — the SaaS hosts reject Authorization: Bearer with HTTP 500, so the connector uses query auth. Endpoints are browser-prefixed (POST): /chrome/content (rendered HTML), /chrome/scrape (CSS-selector extraction), /chrome/screenshot, /chrome/pdf, /chrome/function (run a Puppeteer/Playwright function and return structured JSON), plus /chromium/* and /edge/* equivalents. /content, /scrape, /function (no prefix) are accepted as legacy aliases but the /chrome/* paths are the documented, future-proof ones. All of these only READ data — set `side_effects: "read"` on the request step so dry-run/preview renders them instead of holding them as writes. Rate limiting is concurrency-based.

ENDPOINT SELECTION RULE: Use /chrome/function (not /chrome/content) for any page that loads data via client-side JS after the initial HTML shell arrives. /chrome/content captures whatever has rendered at the moment the browser fires 'load' — for SPAs and server-side-rendered search pages that fetch rows via XHR, this is the empty shell. The page-function you POST to /chrome/function runs INSIDE the external browserless service (not inside subkit), so page.goto, page.waitForSelector, page.evaluate, and all Puppeteer page APIs work exactly as documented. The page-function must return { data: <json>, type: 'application/json' }.

EXACT FUNCTION SHAPE (copy this — getting it wrong is the #1 first-attempt failure): the `code` field MUST be an ES-module DEFAULT EXPORT with this signature — NOT CommonJS:

export default async function ({ page, context }) {
  const { url, selector } = context;
  await page.goto(url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle2' });
  await page.waitForSelector(selector, { timeout: 15000 });
  const data = await page.evaluate((sel) => Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(sel)).map((el) => ({ text: el.textContent.trim() })), selector);
  return { data, type: 'application/json' };
}

Do NOT use module.exports, require(), or import statements, and do NOT wrap it in a plain function — any of those make browserless reject the request with HTTP 400 { "error": "module is not defined" }. Pass runtime inputs (the URL, selectors, filter values) through the POST body's `context` field — NOT by string-interpolating them into the code — and read them as the second destructured argument: { "code": "export default async function ({ page, context }) { … }", "context": { "url": "https://…", "selector": ".row" } }.

RELIABLE SCRAPE PATTERN for pages with late-rendered sections: (1) page.goto(url, { waitUntil: 'networkidle2' }); (2) page.waitForSelector('<primary content selector>', { timeout: 15000 }); (3) verify ALL expected sections are present — if any is absent after the wait, return a structured error object ({ error: 'selector_missing', selector: '...' }) so the workflow step fails explicitly instead of returning partial HTML that silently drops rows; (4) page.evaluate(() => { /* extract and return */ }). For pages with two or more independently-rendered sections (e.g. outbound and return results rendered at different times), poll until ALL are populated: up to 3 page.reload() calls with a page.waitForSelector between each reload, counting rendered rows before proceeding.

STRUCTURED ERRORS: when the page-function cannot find the expected selector, return { error: 'selector_missing', selector: '<selector>', url: page.url() } instead of throwing or returning empty arrays. The workflow can then branch on scope.<step>.data.error and retry or alert. Never return an empty array and let the caller guess whether the scrape succeeded.
